  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The Belgian Photonovel, 1954-1985 shows early scholarly traction and active readership. It has been cited once, and the record also shows 744 downloads, suggesting that the book is being used beyond its initial publication audience. Its subject focus on the photonovel’s cultural history and its alignment with gender equality and education-related themes point to relevance in both research and broader humanities teaching contexts.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Clarissa Colangelo’s monograph traces the Belgian photonovel as a distinctive visual narrative form at the intersection of comics, cinema, literature, and magazine culture. The impact record indicates that the book is already entering scholarly discussion: OpenAlex currently records one citation, appearing in a 2025 article in Contemporary French and Francophone Studies. That citation is an early signal rather than a mature body of uptake, but it does show that the book is being taken up in adjacent humanities scholarship.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence is stronger than the citation count alone might suggest. OAPEN metrics report 744 downloads, which points to sustained reader interest in the open-access version. The monthly pattern includes several higher-use months, indicating that attention has been ongoing rather than a one-time spike. Because downloads measure access and use rather than citation, they should be read as complementary evidence of readership and discovery.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book’s thematic profile also helps explain its reach. Its concepts cluster around comics, narrative, mainstream visual culture, and film-related media, while the associated SDG signal links it to gender equality. Taken together, these markers suggest relevance to scholarship on media history, gendered cultural production, and the circulation of popular print forms. The funding record also notes KU Leuven support, aligning the book with an institutional environment that values humanities research dissemination.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader visibility record is still relatively lean, so the story is best described as emerging rather than fully established. The book is open access through a hybrid OA route, listed in DOAB, and indexed in WorldCat holdings, all of which support discoverability and library access. In short, the evidence points to a specialized scholarly book with clear readership, an initial citation footprint, and topic relevance that gives it a plausible platform for continued academic use.</p>",
